<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · river</p><h1 class="enc-title">River Mersey</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The River Mersey is a river of north-west <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> that flows into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/irish-sea">Irish Sea</a> through a wide estuary at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liverpool">Liverpool</a>. Its name means 'boundary river', and in the early <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a> it formed the frontier between Northumbria and Mercia. The estuary later became one of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a>'s great ports and the heart of the industrial north-west.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/river-mersey.jpg" alt="River Mersey" width="320" height="180" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Rept0n1x · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Liverpool_skyline_from_the_Mersey_Ferry_-_2012-05-27.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
